<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Augustus, Grand Duke of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1757">1757</a>–<a href="/years/1828">1828</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Karl August of Saxe-Weimar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Augustus, Grand Duke of Saxe-Weimar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>, ruled the duchy from 1775, first under his mother Anna Amalia's guidance. He brought Goethe to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> as friend and minister, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herder</a> and Schiller followed, making the small court the centre of German classicism. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> raised him to grand duke, and in 1816 he granted one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s first constitutions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-augustus-grand-duke-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach.jpg" alt="Charles Augustus, Grand Duke of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Christoph Kolbe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gro%C3%9Fherzog_Carl_August_1822.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
